1022 LeetCode Solution in Python:

The problem “Sum of Root To Leaf Binary Numbers” involves a binary tree in which each node contains either 0 or 1.
Every path from the root node to a leaf node represents a binary number formed by concatenating the node values along that path.
The objective is to compute the sum of all such binary numbers converted into their decimal equivalents.

To solve this problem efficiently, a Depth-First Search (DFS) traversal approach is used. DFS explores each path from the root down to the leaves before moving to the next path, making it ideal for problems involving root-to-leaf computations.
The traversal is performed recursively while maintaining a running value that represents the binary number formed so far.
At each step:
Start from the root with an initial value of 0.
Move down the tree, updating the current binary value.
When a leaf node is reached, convert the accumulated binary value to decimal (implicitly during construction) and add it to the total sum.
Continue until all root-to-leaf paths are processed.

Logic Applied
The main logic relies on how binary numbers are formed.
When traversing a path:
Each node contributes one binary digit (0 or 1).
Moving to the next node is equivalent to shifting the existing number left by one bit and inserting the new bit.
Mathematically:
NewValue=(Current Value*2)+Node Value
or using bit manipulation:
NewValue=(Current Value<<1)+Node Value
This operation ensures that the binary number is built incrementally without storing the entire path.
Leaf Node Condition
A node is considered a leaf if it has no left or right children.
When a leaf node is reached:
The current accumulated value represents a complete binary number from root to leaf.
This value is returned and added to the total sum.
Recursion Strategy
The recursive function performs the following:
If the node is null, return 0 (no contribution).
Update the running binary value using the node’s value.
If the node is a leaf, return the current value.
Otherwise, recursively compute values for left and right subtrees.
Return the sum of both recursive calls.

DFS guarantees that every root-to-leaf path is explored exactly once.
Binary construction during traversal avoids extra memory usage for storing paths.
Bit manipulation makes the process efficient and mathematically correct.
Recursion naturally fits the hierarchical structure of trees.
